\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We evaluate on three English IE benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient.
\textbf{SciERC} \cite{luan2018scierc} contains 551 test instances annotated for joint NER (6 types) and RE (7 types).
\textbf{CoNLL-2003} \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} provides 3{,}453 test sentences with 4 entity types, where models achieve ${\approx}$91\% micro F1.
\textbf{WNUT-17} \cite{derczynski2017wnut} consists of 1{,}287 social media instances with emerging entities, used to probe method boundaries (54\% zero F1).

\paragraph{Models and sampling.}
Both \textbf{Qwen3-8B} and \textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct} are fine-tuned with LoRA (rank$=$32, $\alpha{=}64$) for 5 epochs using LLaMA Factory under identical training procedures, ensuring cross-model comparisons reflect architectural differences rather than training confounds.
We draw $N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ (primary), with $N{=}16$ for scaling experiments.\footnote{$N{=}16$ results for CoNLL (Qwen) and SciERC (LLaMA) are in Table~\ref{tab:robustness} (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}).}
Quality estimation uses Spearman $\rho$ and AUROC; downstream utility uses best-of-$N$ selection F1 with exact span matching (NER) and head-tail-label matching (RE).
Inference uses vLLM with constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Quality Estimation Results}
\label{sec:qe_results}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Quality estimation across tasks and models (Spearman $\rho$ and AUROC). Best value per block and column in \textbf{bold}. SciERC evaluates joint NER (6 types) and RE (7 types); CoNLL evaluates NER (4 types). $N{=}8$ results use seed${=}42$; $N{=}16$ results are averaged across seeds \{42, 123, 456\}.}
\label{tab:quality_estimation}
\begin{tabular}{ll cc cc cc cc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC NER}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC RE}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (Qwen)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (LLaMA)}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6} \cmidrule(lr){7-8} \cmidrule(lr){9-10}
Signal & Family & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{10}{l}{\textit{$N{=}8$}} \\
Voting Conf. & Structural & \textbf{.379} & .684 & \textbf{.350} & .693 & .424 & .789 & .426 & .709 \\
Soft Jaccard  & Structural & .360 & .692 & .250 & .670 & .436 & .801 & \textbf{.431} & .704 \\
Fleiss' $\kappa$ & Surface & .267 & .629 & .275 & .609 & .400 & .774 & .428 & .703 \\
Exact Match  & Surface    & .295 & \textbf{.749} & .134 & \textbf{.706} & \textbf{.451} & \textbf{.816} & \textbf{.431} & .705 \\
Log-prob     & Model      & .205 & .578 & .266 & .600 & .316 & .753 & .307 & \textbf{.761} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{10}{l}{\textit{$N{=}16$ (3-seed)}} \\
Voting Conf. & Structural & & & & & & & .458${\pm}$.003 & \\
Soft Jaccard  & Structural & & & & & & & \textbf{.463${\pm}$.007} & \\
Fleiss' $\kappa$ & Surface & & & & & & & .455${\pm}$.006 & \\
Exact Match  & Surface    & & & & & & & .460${\pm}$.008 & \\
Log-prob     & Model      & & & & & & & .310${\pm}$.005 & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_signal_heatmap}
\caption{Quality estimation heatmap (Spearman $\rho$) across five signals and four configurations. Left: full evaluation set; right: conditional set (non-zero F1 instances). On the full set, structural signals dominate on SciERC while all signals converge on CoNLL. On the conditional set, EM emerges as the strongest signal on both NER ($\rho{=}0.360$) and RE ($\rho{=}0.417$), while logprob RE collapses to near-zero ($\rho{=}0.011$).}
\label{fig:signal_heatmap}
\end{figure*}

Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation} presents rank correlation ($\rho$) and AUROC for five confidence signals across tasks and models.

\paragraph{Structural signals dominate on complex schemas.}
On SciERC NER, Voting achieves $\rho{=}0.379$ and SJ $\rho{=}0.360$, both outperforming surface ($\rho{\leq}0.295$) and logprob ($\rho{=}0.205$).
The structural advantage persists on RE (Voting $\rho{=}0.350$), though all signals weaken due to the 44\% zero-F1 floor effect.
Entity voting and SJ show moderate instance-level correlation (NER: $\rho{=}0.659$; RE: $\rho{=}0.605$), indicating information overlap but not redundancy; their differential behavior across difficulty levels reflects the complementary granularity of entity-level frequency vs.\ span-level set overlap.
While the best $\rho{=}0.38$ (Voting) explains approximately 14\% of F1 variance, the best AUROC reaches 0.749 (EM), indicating strong binary discriminative power for the quality/failure classification most relevant to practitioners in quality-aware routing.\footnote{For context, semantic entropy achieves AUROC $\approx$ 0.75--0.85 on discrete-answer QA tasks \cite{kuhn2023semantic}; direct comparison is complicated by the fundamentally different output spaces of structured IE versus closed-form QA.}
Bootstrap CIs for signal pair differences on SciERC NER ($N{=}8$): SJ$-$logprob $\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.155$ [+0.074, +0.234]; Voting$-$SJ $\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.019$ [$-$0.033, +0.073]; SJ$-$EM $\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.065$ [+0.003, +0.130].
The SJ advantage over logprob is significant, while the Voting--SJ gap is not; the SJ--EM difference is marginal (CI lower bound +0.003; see Table~\ref{tab:bootstrap_signal_ci}, Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal} for individual signal CIs).

\paragraph{$\rho$--AUROC dissociation.}
On NER, EM leads AUROC (0.749) despite ranking third in $\rho$ (0.295); on RE, EM achieves the highest AUROC (0.706) while ranking last in $\rho$ (0.134).
Binary agreement provides clean separation between reliable and unreliable instances, while structural signals capture finer-grained quality variation.
On CoNLL with LLaMA, logprob achieves the highest AUROC (0.761) yet the lowest $\rho$ (0.307), confirming this dissociation across datasets.

\paragraph{Signal rankings shift with task difficulty.}
On CoNLL (greedy F1${\approx}$0.91), EM leads ($\rho{=}0.451$, AUROC${=}0.816$), surpassing SJ and Voting.
When most samples are near-correct, binary agreement captures remaining variance more efficiently than set-level similarity.
This reversal---EM${>}$SJ on easy tasks vs.\ SJ${>}$EM on hard tasks---demonstrates that optimal signal choice depends on task difficulty.
Cross-model stability is strong: SJ achieves $\rho{=}0.436$ (Qwen) vs.\ $0.431$ (LLaMA) on CoNLL ($\Delta{=}0.005$, within 3-seed $\sigma{\approx}0.007$).
On WNUT-17, where 54\% of instances receive zero F1, full-set correlations are misleading: voting achieves $\rho{=}0.724$ (AUROC${=}0.913$), but this is entirely driven by the zero/non-zero boundary (conditional $\rho{=}0.038$, n.s.; Table~\ref{tab:wnut}).
Logprob exhibits strong \textit{negative} correlation ($\rho{=}{-}0.410$, AUROC${=}0.219$), indicating that higher model confidence associates with lower extraction quality on this noisy domain---further undermining logprob as a general-purpose IE quality signal.
On the conditional set, all signals collapse toward weak or non-significant correlations ($\rho{\leq}0.35$), with SJ achieving only $\rho{=}0.089$ ($p{=}0.020$) on the full set---suggesting a capability boundary beyond which consistency signals capture binary success/failure but not quality gradation.

\paragraph{Conditional filtering.}
Filtering to non-zero F1 instances reshuffles rankings (Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap}, right).
EM emerges as the strongest conditional signal on both NER ($\rho{=}0.360$) and RE ($\rho{=}0.417$), while Voting and FK drop substantially (Voting NER: $0.379{\to}0.300$; FK NER: $0.267{\to}0.176$).
Logprob RE collapses to $\rho{=}0.011$, confirming its full-set correlation was driven by zero/non-zero separation.
This creates a natural complementarity: Voting and SJ are preferred on the full set, while EM provides the most precise ranking within the non-trivial subset (see Appendix~\ref{app:additional} for details).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{The Correlation-Selection Gap}
\label{sec:selection_gap}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_selection_gap}
\caption{The correlation-selection gap on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Left: NER; right: RE. Bars show best-of-$N$ selection F1; the line marks quality estimation $\rho$. Despite $\rho$ up to 0.38, no signal reliably surpasses greedy on either task ($p{=}0.39$ for NER logprob, the best signal). Dashed line indicates oracle upper bound.}
\label{fig:selection_gap}
\end{figure*}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Best-of-$N$ selection F1 on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Best non-oracle result per column in \textbf{bold}. EM is omitted because exact-match plurality is degenerate in structured IE: with $N{=}8$, the probability of two identical structured outputs is negligible.}
\label{tab:selection}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & NER F1 & RE F1 \\
\midrule
Oracle & 0.776 & 0.585 \\
\midrule
Log-prob           & \textbf{0.650} & 0.395 \\
Soft Jaccard       & 0.645 & 0.386 \\
Voting             & 0.634 & 0.359 \\
Exact Match        & ---   & ---   \\
\midrule
Greedy ($T{=}0$)   & 0.644 & \textbf{0.404} \\
Random             & 0.608 & 0.354 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Despite strong quality estimation, confidence signals yield limited gains when used for best-of-$N$ selection (Table~\ref{tab:selection}, Figure~\ref{fig:selection_gap}).

\paragraph{No signal reliably surpasses greedy.}
We exclude 22 instances with empty gold annotations, yielding $n{=}529$ for statistical analysis.
We evaluate statistical significance using instance-level paired bootstrap resampling ($B{=}10{,}000$, with replacement) over these instances, reporting 95\% percentile CIs.
The three random seeds (42, 123, 456) refer to independent inference runs; we average $\rho$ across seeds and report the bootstrap $p$-value of the paired difference (best signal vs.\ greedy).
On NER, the oracle (0.776) reveals 13.2~pp of headroom above greedy (0.644).
Logprob achieves the highest selection F1 (0.650, +0.6~pp), but this is not statistically significant ($p{=}0.39$; 95\% CI: [$-$3.0, +4.2]~pp; Cohen's $d{=}0.038$; sign test: 61W/70L, win\%${=}$46.6\%).
On RE, the gap is complete: the best signal (logprob, 0.395) falls below greedy (0.404, $-0.9$\,pp, $p{=}0.80$), while 18.1~pp of oracle headroom remains.
Voting ($\rho{=}0.379$, the best quality estimator) selects to only 0.634 on NER---\textit{below} greedy.
The correlation-selection gap is consistent across all six tested configurations (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}), with no signal significantly outperforming greedy in any setting.

\paragraph{Quality estimation $\neq$ output selection.}
Instance-level confidence signals predict \textit{which inputs} the model handles reliably, not \textit{which output} among $N$ candidates is best.
When per-sample variance is low relative to instance-level difficulty, even perfect quality estimation cannot discriminate among near-identical candidates.
This gap has direct consequences for test-time scaling in IE \cite{brown2024monkeys, snell2024scaling}: closing it likely requires per-sample signals such as trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers}.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}
\label{sec:robustness_main}

Consistency-based signals (SJ, Voting, FK) show robust performance across $T{\in}\{0.7, 1.0\}$; logprob temperature sensitivity remains uncharacterized due to unavailable $T{=}0.7$ data (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}, Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}).
SJ NER $\rho$ shifts by only 0.028 between $T{=}0.7$ and $T{=}1.0$ (bootstrap CI includes zero).
Doubling from $N{=}8$ to $N{=}16$ yields sublinear gains: SJ improves by $+0.052$ on Qwen SciERC NER, while logprob is approximately $N$-invariant ($\Delta\rho{\leq}0.021$).
The structural-over-logprob advantage is consistent across tested model families, holding across all four $N{=}16$ settings ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.135$ to ${+}0.175$).
NER signals are reasonably well-calibrated (ECE 0.069--0.096; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}), while RE exhibits greater miscalibration (ECE 0.109--0.339).
For RE at $N{=}16$, SJ, FK, and logprob all plateau at $\rho{\approx}0.28$, suggesting compound errors impose a ceiling.
